WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.842
Entity design is part of defensive application architecture.

2
00:00:03.842 --> 00:00:08.580
Persistence entities describe how the application stores and relates data.

3
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:15.431
API input models, view models, and command models describe what a caller or workflow is allowed to provide.

4
00:00:15.431 --> 00:00:23.755
Keeping those models separate where it improves safety helps prevent external input from shaping more of the entity than intended.

5
00:00:23.755 --> 00:00:27.981
Over-posting and unintended field updates are common design risks.

6
00:00:27.981 --> 00:00:38.674
A request to update a customer display name should not also allow changes to account ownership, tenant ID, pricing tier, approval state, role state, or audit metadata.

7
00:00:38.674 --> 00:00:45.846
Operation-specific models and explicit mapping make it clearer which properties can be changed by each workflow.

8
00:00:45.846 --> 00:00:50.264
Navigation properties and relationship changes need ownership review.

9
00:00:50.264 --> 00:00:55.002
Updating a relationship can be as sensitive as updating a scalar property.

10
00:00:55.002 --> 00:01:07.168
A caller should not be able to attach records to another tenant, move ownership, alter membership, or change a workflow relationship simply because an entity graph can represent that change.

11
00:01:07.168 --> 00:01:08.833
Tracking behavior matters.

12
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:21.062
Tracked queries are useful when the application intends to modify entities, but they also make updates easier to perform accidentally if broad object graphs are attached or mapped carelessly.

13
00:01:21.062 --> 00:01:25.417
No-tracking queries can reduce overhead and clarify read-only paths.

14
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:28.554
The choice should match intent rather than habit.

15
00:01:28.554 --> 00:01:33.100
Concurrency tokens and conflict handling are practical defensive tools.

16
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:41.296
They help the application notice when data changed between read and write, so updates do not silently overwrite important state.

17
00:01:41.296 --> 00:01:48.147
Conflict handling should be designed around the business workflow, user experience, and audit expectations.

18
00:01:48.147 --> 00:01:50.773
Protect server-owned fields deliberately.

19
00:01:50.773 --> 00:02:02.682
Ownership, tenant identifiers, approval state, role assignments, pricing, status transitions, audit fields, and security-relevant flags should be changed only through reviewed workflows.

20
00:02:02.682 --> 00:02:11.454
EF Core mapping is powerful, so the application must be explicit about which parts of the entity are caller-controlled and which are not.
